Once 28 makes a pattern the way a value is checked, the hardcoded checks in
info_faults are the odd ones out. Today they are greps written by hand, one
per line, and they are weaker than what the project gets for its own fields:

  sed -n 1p | grep -q '^claim:'      anything after the colon passes
  sed -n 2p | grep -q '^needs:'      anything after the colon passes
  sed -n 3p | grep -q '^title: .'    any non-empty rest of line
  created / updated                  an ISO-8601 pattern, inline in the code

So claim:garbage is a valid ticket and needs: yesterday is caught only
later, indirectly, by the dependency resolver failing to find a ticket
called "yesterday". The project can say effort must match S|M|L; pidly
cannot say what its own claim line looks like.

Express them the same way. The claim grammar is the one that earns it:

  empty
  <pid>.<starttime>@<runtime>
  <pid>@<runtime>                    no start time available
  <agent>@- <expiry>                 a lease

which is a pattern, and one that would have caught every malformed claim the
tests fabricate by hand.

The design question is whose patterns they are. These lines are pidly's, not
the project's, so a project must not be able to weaken them — a schema that
let title be empty would break every listing. Options: a built-in stanza set
compiled into the script that check always applies and the project's schema
cannot override; or seeded stanzas that the project can tighten but not
loosen, which needs a rule for what "tighten" means and is probably not
worth it. The first is likely right, and it puts the format's grammar in one
readable place instead of scattered across check.

Watch the ordering: claim, needs and title are lines 1, 2 and 3 by position,
not by name, so their stanzas need to say so — a pattern alone does not
express "this is the first line". That is the one place position is real,
because the takeable scan depends on it.

Needs 28 for the pattern machinery, the ERE dialect decision and the lint
that keeps a permissive grep from hiding a bad pattern.

Done when the structural lines and created, updated and handoff are checked
against declared patterns, the project cannot weaken them, a malformed claim
is a finding, and the tests cover each line being wrong in its own way.
